multichat.db

# ANCHORED. This rule exists for the stale ELF binary at the repo root, but written
# bare as "server" it also matched cmd/server/ -- THE MAIN PACKAGE'S OWN SOURCE
# DIRECTORY -- because an unanchored pattern matches at every level. cmd/server/main.go
# survives only because it was tracked before the rule existed; the next file added to
# the main package would have been silently ignored, never shipped, and the committed
# tree would no longer build while `git status` looked perfectly clean. A "!*.go"
# exception cannot rescue this: git will not re-include a file inside an excluded
# directory. Anchoring is the only fix.
/server

# The compiled server. It was COMMITTED as a 13 MB ELF binary alongside its own
# source until 2026-07-13, so every build left the tree dirty and invited someone to
# commit the new one. Anchored with a leading slash on purpose: a bare
# "multichat-server" would also match a directory of that name anywhere in the tree.
/multichat-server

# The .openclaw/ subtree is a committed OpenClaw agent workspace. Whether its docs
# (AGENTS.md, SOUL.md, ...) belong in this repo is a judgment call left to the owner,
# but two things inside it are build artifacts that never belong in git under any
# resolution of that call, so they were untracked 2026-07-16:
#   - the vendored frontend node_modules (esbuild/rollup ELF binaries + 10k JS files)
#   - the compiled .openclaw/server ELF binary
# Both anchored to the subtree root so the rules can't leak to a same-named path
# elsewhere in the tree. Files remain on disk; only the git tracking was removed.
/.openclaw/frontend/node_modules/
/.openclaw/server

# deploy.sh builds the frontend into this folder BEFORE it runs `go build`, and only
# swaps it into place afterwards. Unignored, git counts it as an untracked file at
# build time, so Go stamps every deployed binary vcs.modified=true even when HEAD is
# clean -- and a clean deploy becomes indistinguishable from one built off a dirty
# tree. Anchored so it cannot match a same-named folder elsewhere.
/frontend/.dist-staging/
